# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene in Matthew. Matthew 24 opens as Jesus leaves the temple. “And Jesus went out, and departed from the temple: and his disciples came to him for to shew him the buildings of the temple” (24:1). Jesus answers, “See ye not all these things? verily I say unto you, There shall not be left here one stone upon another, that shall not be thrown down” (24:2). Then comes the question: “And as he sat upon the mount of Olives, the disciples came unto him privately, saying, Tell us, when shall these things be? and what shall be the sign of thy coming, and of the end of the world?” (24:3).'))
A(('p', '2.2 The scene in Mark. Mark records a remark about the building: “Master, see what manner of stones and what buildings are here!” (13:1), and the same answer about the stones (13:2). Mark then names who asks: “And as he sat upon the mount of Olives over against the temple, Peter and James and John and Andrew asked him privately, Tell us, when shall these things be? and what shall be the sign when all these things shall be fulfilled?” (13:3–4).'))
A(('p', '2.3 The scene in Luke. Luke records that “some spake of the temple, how it was adorned with goodly stones and gifts” (21:5), and Jesus’ answer about the stones (21:6). Then: “And they asked him, saying, Master, but when shall these things be? and what sign will there be when these things shall come to pass?” (21:7). Luke does not name those who ask. Luke records that Jesus was “teaching in the temple” by day and that He “abode in the mount that is called the mount of Olives” at night (21:37).'))
A(('call', 'The Question Behind the Fig Tree',
   'In each account a question about the temple and about “these things” comes first. Matthew adds “the sign of thy coming, and of the end of the world” (24:3). Mark asks for “the sign when all these things shall be fulfilled” (13:4). Luke asks for “what sign will there be when these things shall come to pass” (21:7). The fig tree lesson is told in the answer that follows. Jesus does not say which of its parts answers which part of the question.'))
A(('p', '2.4 Where the lesson stands. In each account the lesson comes after Jesus has spoken at length in answer to the question, and before a saying about the day and hour. Matthew has “But of that day and hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels of heaven, but my Father only” (24:36). Mark has “But of that day and that hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels which are in heaven, neither the Son, but the Father” (13:32). Luke goes on with “And take heed to yourselves, lest at any time your hearts be overcharged with surfeiting, and drunkenness, and cares of this life, and so that day come upon you unawares” (21:34). The lesson is a short unit inside a longer discourse, and the discourse around it is its setting and not its subject.'))
A(('p', '2.5 The prophetic context. Matthew has said what Jesus preached: “From that time Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). Mark records, “The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand: repent ye, and believe the gospel” (1:15). Jesus sent the Twelve with a limited charge: “Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (Matthew 10:5–6). The fig tree lesson is spoken to disciples who had heard this preaching.'))
A(('p', '2.6 Why Jesus tells it here. None of the three accounts states why Jesus chose a fig tree. Matthew and Mark both introduce it with the same words, “Now learn a parable of the fig tree.” Luke introduces it with “And he spake to them a parable; Behold the fig tree, and all the trees.” The text gives the place, the question and what follows. It does not give a reason beyond these.'))
