A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The comparison is read here in the order Jesus gives it, one account at a time. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another account or another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 Matthew 24:32–35. “Now learn a parable of the fig tree; When his branch is yet tender, and putteth forth leaves, ye know that summer is nigh.” The comparison opens with a command to learn. It names a fig tree, its branch, the branch as “yet tender,” and the putting forth of leaves. What follows is what “ye know”: “that summer is nigh.”'))
A(('p', '3.2 Matthew 24:33. “So likewise ye, when ye shall see all these things, know that it is near, even at the doors.” Jesus turns to the hearers with “So likewise ye.” They are to “see all these things” and “know that it is near, even at the doors.” The verse is recorded in Section 4 as Jesus’ own application.'))
A(('p', '3.3 Matthew 24:34–35. “Verily I say unto you, This generation shall not pass, till all these things be fulfilled. Heaven and earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away.” Two sayings follow, each with its own weight. The first says that “this generation shall not pass” until “all these things be fulfilled.” The second says that heaven and earth shall pass away and His words shall not.'))
A(('p', '3.4 Mark 13:28–31. “Now learn a parable of the fig tree; When her branch is yet tender, and putteth forth leaves, ye know that summer is near.” Mark’s opening is the same as Matthew’s, with “her branch” and “summer is near.” Then: “So ye in like manner, when ye shall see these things come to pass, know that it is nigh, even at the doors.” Then: “Verily I say unto you, that this generation shall not pass, till all these things be done. Heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away.”'))
A(('p', '3.5 Luke 21:29–33. “And he spake to them a parable; Behold the fig tree, and all the trees; When they now shoot forth, ye see and know of your own selves that summer is now nigh at hand.” Luke names “the fig tree, and all the trees,” and says they “shoot forth.” The hearers “see and know of your own selves” that summer is “now nigh at hand.” Then: “So likewise ye, when ye see these things come to pass, know ye that the kingdom of God is nigh at hand.” Then: “Verily I say unto you, This generation shall not pass away, till all be fulfilled. Heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away.”'))
A(('p', '3.6 The three accounts side by side, by wording only. The table sets the same moments of the three accounts in a row. It records what each verse says and nothing more.'))
