A(('p', '3.7 What the comparison contains. The text names a fig tree, a branch that is tender, leaves, summer, hearers who see and know, and a nearness described as “at the doors” or “at hand.” In Luke it also names “all the trees.” The action is one of seeing a change in a tree and knowing from it that a season is near, and then, in Jesus’ application, seeing certain things and knowing that something is near.'))
A(('p', '3.8 What the comparison leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say what the fig tree is. Jesus does not say what the branch, the tenderness or the leaves are. Jesus does not say what summer is. Jesus does not say what “all these things” or “these things” includes, and He does not say whether the phrase has the same range in each account. Jesus does not say what “it” is in Matthew 24:33 and Mark 13:29. Jesus does not say how near “near,” “nigh,” “at the doors” and “at hand” are. Jesus does not say what “this generation” is. Jesus does not say what “all” is in Luke 21:32. The comparison stops at the application and at the saying about His words.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 What Jesus gives. Jesus gives no explanation of the fig tree. He does not say what the tree, the branch or the leaves stand for, and no request for one is recorded. What He does give, in each account, is one sentence of application that begins “So likewise ye” or “So ye in like manner.” That sentence is recorded here word for word, as the three accounts give it. It identifies nothing beyond what it says.'))
A(('p', '**Jesus’ own words of application.** Matthew 24:33: “So likewise ye, when ye shall see all these things, know that it is near, even at the doors.” Mark 13:29: “So ye in like manner, when ye shall see these things come to pass, know that it is nigh, even at the doors.” Luke 21:31: “So likewise ye, when ye see these things come to pass, know ye that the kingdom of God is nigh at hand.” The three sentences are recorded as written. They are not combined into one.'))
A(('p', '4.2 The three sentences compared. Matthew says “when ye shall see all these things” and “know that it is near.” Mark says “when ye shall see these things come to pass” and “know that it is nigh.” Luke says “when ye see these things come to pass” and “know ye that the kingdom of God is nigh at hand.” The differences are these. Matthew has “all these things”; Mark and Luke have “these things come to pass.” Matthew has “near” and Mark has “nigh”; Luke has “nigh at hand.” Matthew and Mark have “it”; Luke has “the kingdom of God.” Matthew and Mark add “even at the doors”; Luke does not. The text does not say that the three sentences name one and the same thing, and none of them is read into another.'))
A(('p', '4.3 The sayings about “this generation.” In Matthew 24:34 Jesus says “This generation shall not pass, till all these things be fulfilled.” In Mark 13:30 He says “this generation shall not pass, till all these things be done.” In Luke 21:32 He says “This generation shall not pass away, till all be fulfilled.” Each is introduced with “Verily I say unto you.” Each is recorded here as written. Jesus does not say which generation He means, and He does not say which things are to be fulfilled or done. The sayings are not turned into a date.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What Jesus does not identify. Jesus does not identify the fig tree. Jesus does not identify the branch, its tenderness or its leaves. Jesus does not identify summer. Jesus does not identify “all the trees” in Luke 21:29. Jesus does not identify “all these things” or “these things.” Jesus does not identify “it” in Matthew 24:33 and Mark 13:29. Jesus does not identify “this generation,” and He does not identify what is “fulfilled” or “done.” In Luke 21:31 He names “the kingdom of God” as nigh at hand and says no more of it in that verse.'))
