# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9
A(('h1', S9))
A(('bul', [
    '**Between the three accounts.** Matthew has “his branch,” “all these things” and “it is near.” Mark has “her branch,” “these things come to pass” and “it is nigh.” Luke has “all the trees,” “shoot forth” and “the kingdom of God is nigh at hand.” Each is recorded as written and none is merged.',
    '**Between the comparison and the application.** The tree, the branch and the leaves belong to the comparison. “Ye … know that it is near” belongs to Jesus’ application. Jesus does not say how the one maps onto the other.',
    '**Between nearness and the day and hour.** The lesson speaks of what is near. The saying that follows speaks of a day and hour no man knows. The one is not made the other.',
    '**Between this lesson and its neighbors.** The leaves and summer belong to this lesson. The fruit sought and not found belongs to the Barren Fig Tree. The delaying servant belongs to the Faithful and Unfaithful Servant. The lamps belong to the Ten Virgins.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the fig tree, the branch, the leaves, summer, “all the trees,” “all these things,” “it” or “this generation.” The Body of Christ is none of them.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from the Barren Fig Tree, the Faithful and Unfaithful Servant, the Ten Virgins, the Thief and Watchful Servants, or any other place that uses the word “generation.”',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Seeing and knowing are actions in a comparison. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
]))

