A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Barren Fig Tree, the Faithful and Unfaithful Servant, the Ten Virgins and the Thief and Watchful Servants are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. This lesson makes no use of their meanings, and they make none of this one.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus tells His disciples to learn from the fig tree: when its branch is tender and puts forth leaves, they know that summer is near. He then tells them that when they see the things He names, they are to know that it is near, even at the doors, and in Luke that the kingdom of God is nigh at hand. He says that this generation shall not pass until all is fulfilled or done, and that heaven and earth shall pass away but His words shall not. He gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that what is seen tells what is near, that the hearers are told to know, and that His words do not pass away.'))
